WEBVTT
Kind: captions
Language: en

00:00:01.240 --> 00:00:04.100
- You can go days without food,

00:00:05.660 --> 00:00:08.280
traverse unimaginable distances,

00:00:09.440 --> 00:00:12.000
endure relentless blizzards.

00:00:13.060 --> 00:00:15.080
But if you're a wolf on
the edge of the Arctic,

00:00:15.340 --> 00:00:17.160
up against the biggest predator,

00:00:17.680 --> 00:00:20.260
there's one thing you can't do without...

00:00:20.420 --> 00:00:22.960
(dramatic music)

00:00:23.080 --> 00:00:23.920
The pack.

00:00:27.540 --> 00:00:31.800
(dramatic music)

00:00:31.880 --> 00:00:35.740
(wind blowing)

00:00:36.060 --> 00:00:40.480
At first glance, the Arctic
feels brutally devoid of life,

00:00:40.920 --> 00:00:42.540
though a select group of animals

00:00:42.680 --> 00:00:44.840
have figured out a way to thrive.

00:00:45.600 --> 00:00:47.300
(seal howling)

00:00:47.440 --> 00:00:50.840
But the real force here
works quietly underfoot.

00:00:51.380 --> 00:00:55.000
Every winter, sea ice
starts small and delicate.

00:00:55.220 --> 00:00:58.080
Then it grows over
millions of square miles.

00:00:58.080 --> 00:01:00.280
How is it possible that this freezer

00:01:00.280 --> 00:01:02.440
holds the key to our entire planet?

00:01:02.980 --> 00:01:05.620
Like a lung, sea ice breathes life

00:01:05.620 --> 00:01:07.480
into the Arctic every year,

00:01:07.480 --> 00:01:10.820
in a cycle that impacts the
climate across the globe.

00:01:11.600 --> 00:01:14.580
This is the story from the
edge of the Canadian Arctic

00:01:14.640 --> 00:01:16.240
where it all starts.

00:01:16.240 --> 00:01:17.540
(dramatic music)

00:01:18.140 --> 00:01:19.460
My name's Bertie Gregory, and I'm a

00:01:19.460 --> 00:01:21.860
National Geographic wildlife filmmaker.

00:01:23.040 --> 00:01:24.780
Everything is at stake.

00:01:24.940 --> 00:01:27.540
We're all waiting for The Big Freeze.

00:01:33.820 --> 00:01:39.580
(solemn music)

00:01:40.240 --> 00:01:42.520
This is gray wolf territory,

00:01:42.520 --> 00:01:46.280
but right now it's also a
polar bear migration path,

00:01:46.280 --> 00:01:48.240
and until the sea freezes over,

00:01:48.240 --> 00:01:49.580
these two mighty predators

00:01:49.580 --> 00:01:51.940
will have to share this coastline.

00:01:55.980 --> 00:02:00.160
We've received a tip-off about
a very cool pack of wolves,

00:02:00.180 --> 00:02:02.640
and reportedly they've
been seen harassing,

00:02:02.640 --> 00:02:04.600
or even hunting polar bears.

00:02:05.000 --> 00:02:06.560
This has never been filmed before,

00:02:06.580 --> 00:02:08.880
so we're buzzing there
now to see if we can

00:02:08.960 --> 00:02:10.320
try and catch up with them.

00:02:16.920 --> 00:02:19.260
Wolves here are always on the move.

00:02:19.260 --> 00:02:21.360
They live in such a harsh environment

00:02:21.360 --> 00:02:24.820
that they roam up to 1000
square miles in search of prey.

00:02:27.680 --> 00:02:29.340
Hey, look.

00:02:31.320 --> 00:02:34.140
We don't want to spook him.

00:02:34.280 --> 00:02:37.360
He's coming right up to us right now.

00:02:40.520 --> 00:02:41.980
That's a pretty bold wolf.

00:02:41.980 --> 00:02:44.060
(laughs)

00:02:45.320 --> 00:02:46.940
I've spent months
looking for wolves before

00:02:46.940 --> 00:02:47.960
and never seen one.

00:02:48.900 --> 00:02:52.580
- Yeah, so much to going to
look for them. (laughing)

00:03:00.720 --> 00:03:01.220
- Wolf.

00:03:01.220 --> 00:03:04.080
Over the snow drift, just on the right.

00:03:04.360 --> 00:03:06.140
He's going for the bear.

00:03:06.200 --> 00:03:08.160
(dramatic music)

00:03:08.160 --> 00:03:11.420
Polar bears are the apex
predators of the Arctic.

00:03:11.740 --> 00:03:14.000
Weighing up to 1500 pounds,

00:03:14.000 --> 00:03:16.240
no one dares touch them,

00:03:16.240 --> 00:03:18.840
except the wolves we're tracking.

00:03:20.680 --> 00:03:22.320
If they can catch this bear,

00:03:22.320 --> 00:03:24.140
it would feed the pack for a week.

00:03:24.140 --> 00:03:28.060
But this bear is not having it.

00:03:32.940 --> 00:03:34.840
(growling)

00:03:34.840 --> 00:03:36.120
There's another one.

00:03:36.120 --> 00:03:38.260
- [Man] There's three,
there's a third one coming.

00:03:39.320 --> 00:03:42.380
- A big pack of wolves
could take down a polar bear

00:03:42.380 --> 00:03:44.360
but with only three against one,

00:03:44.580 --> 00:03:47.280
who is predator and who is prey?

00:03:48.220 --> 00:03:57.600
(dramatic music)

00:03:58.000 --> 00:03:59.720
The bear runs the numbers,

00:04:01.080 --> 00:04:02.080
and goes for it.

00:04:02.080 --> 00:04:02.800
(growling)

00:04:03.220 --> 00:04:13.020
(dramatic music)

00:04:13.460 --> 00:04:15.660
That is not a chase you see every day.

00:04:15.660 --> 00:04:18.160
Imagine chasing after a polar bear,

00:04:18.160 --> 00:04:20.800
a massive, really dangerous predator.

00:04:20.920 --> 00:04:22.360
When is that ever a good thing

00:04:22.420 --> 00:04:23.360
that you should try and eat?

00:04:29.180 --> 00:04:31.900
Finding these wolves is
gonna be a real challenge.

00:04:31.900 --> 00:04:34.300
You know, even though it
was here this morning,

00:04:34.300 --> 00:04:35.760
this afternoon it could
be 20 miles that way,

00:04:35.760 --> 00:04:38.280
it could be 20 miles that way.

00:04:38.280 --> 00:04:41.260
And I guess we just gotta
get really, really lucky.

00:04:41.260 --> 00:04:44.500
(motor revving)

00:04:44.680 --> 00:04:45.640
We're pretty stuck.

00:04:51.780 --> 00:04:53.680
Got ourselves a pretty good one.

00:05:01.480 --> 00:05:02.120
Yeah.

00:05:08.560 --> 00:05:10.280
We haven't found any wolves,

00:05:11.460 --> 00:05:13.700
but we have found some
of their favorite food.

00:05:13.700 --> 00:05:14.720
There's at least one moose.

00:05:14.720 --> 00:05:16.800
Isn't it amazing?

00:05:17.940 --> 00:05:21.100
Don't spook, don't spook, don't spook.

00:05:22.600 --> 00:05:24.600
He's got a massive rack.

00:05:29.280 --> 00:05:31.980
Oh, he's coming over, backing up.

00:05:35.320 --> 00:05:37.760
It's like a giant horse on steroids.

00:05:39.280 --> 00:05:40.140
(laughter)

00:05:44.580 --> 00:05:46.060
(dramatic music)

00:05:46.340 --> 00:05:48.360
It's our ninth day searching.

00:05:48.360 --> 00:05:49.780
Still no wolf pack.

00:05:50.020 --> 00:05:51.980
They just keep eluding us.

00:05:52.100 --> 00:06:02.540
(dramatic music)

00:06:02.540 --> 00:06:04.020
- [Man] I'm gonna go up
the tree and have a look.

00:06:05.820 --> 00:06:10.620
(dramatic music)

00:06:10.620 --> 00:06:13.340
- Well, we found the
wolves, and they're asleep,

00:06:13.700 --> 00:06:16.660
so we're trying to sneak
in so they don't wake up.

00:06:21.560 --> 00:06:24.740
This has gotta be the most
unstealthy approach ever.

00:06:27.160 --> 00:06:28.360
We've actually got really lucky.

00:06:28.360 --> 00:06:31.500
We're downwind of them,
and their sense of smell

00:06:31.500 --> 00:06:33.360
is about 100 times better than us,

00:06:33.360 --> 00:06:35.240
so if we were upwind they'd know

00:06:35.240 --> 00:06:36.760
what we had for breakfast,

00:06:36.760 --> 00:06:38.840
they'd know the last time we showered,

00:06:38.840 --> 00:06:40.360
which wasn't very recently.

00:06:40.360 --> 00:06:41.960
They'd know everything about us.

00:06:48.220 --> 00:06:53.720
(howling)

00:06:53.720 --> 00:06:59.500
(howling)

00:06:59.500 --> 00:07:02.960
You can hear, they all
howl at different pitches.

00:07:02.960 --> 00:07:05.620
They're almost like a wild edition

00:07:05.620 --> 00:07:06.900
of an a cappella group.

00:07:08.340 --> 00:07:10.720
And even if you get a new wolf in,

00:07:10.720 --> 00:07:13.840
the new wolf to the pack will
adjust the pitch in its howl

00:07:13.840 --> 00:07:15.360
to the rest of the pack to make sure

00:07:15.360 --> 00:07:17.240
it's not copying one of them.

00:07:17.520 --> 00:07:19.680
(howling)

00:07:28.040 --> 00:07:31.320
There's a big male,
must be the alpha male.

00:07:31.320 --> 00:07:33.800
He seems to be calling the shots,

00:07:34.020 --> 00:07:37.340
and he's got this big chunk
out the side of his face.

00:07:37.420 --> 00:07:39.080
He's running with a bit of a limp.

00:07:39.080 --> 00:07:40.180
You know, one of the
reasons they live in packs

00:07:40.180 --> 00:07:41.780
is 'cause if they get injured,

00:07:42.120 --> 00:07:44.520
the rest of the pack can
still provide for them.

00:07:46.380 --> 00:07:49.420
Wolf packs are also led
by the alpha female.

00:07:51.940 --> 00:07:54.540
Together they decide
where and when to hunt.

00:07:55.400 --> 00:07:57.940
This pair are also the only ones to breed,

00:07:58.080 --> 00:08:00.520
adding new pups to the pack each year.

00:08:05.280 --> 00:08:06.300
They're coming over.

00:08:18.680 --> 00:08:20.540
In the movies and in the media,

00:08:20.540 --> 00:08:23.380
we like to demonize these animals.

00:08:23.380 --> 00:08:26.040
But despite being just a few feet away,

00:08:26.040 --> 00:08:28.640
I never felt any threat in those eyes,

00:08:28.720 --> 00:08:30.020
just curiosity.

00:08:32.280 --> 00:08:34.940
They hung out with us
like this for over an hour

00:08:35.740 --> 00:08:38.240
until the alpha female called the pack

00:08:38.240 --> 00:08:39.420
and off they went.

00:08:39.420 --> 00:08:42.500
(howling)

00:08:43.740 --> 00:08:44.620
Time to hunt.

00:08:48.520 --> 00:08:52.000
There used to be two million
wolves in North America,

00:08:52.000 --> 00:08:55.100
but 400 years of systematic extermination

00:08:55.140 --> 00:08:57.700
has left us with just 80,000.

00:08:59.860 --> 00:09:01.920
Wild places depend on these wolves,

00:09:02.020 --> 00:09:04.340
as they regulate prey populations.

00:09:05.540 --> 00:09:08.040
We must give them the
chance to bounce back.

00:09:09.620 --> 00:09:11.480
Each hunt follows a strategy.

00:09:12.300 --> 00:09:14.420
First they close in.

00:09:15.200 --> 00:09:19.000
(dramatic music)

00:09:19.000 --> 00:09:19.840
Ready?

00:09:20.480 --> 00:09:22.860
(dramatic music)

00:09:22.860 --> 00:09:23.720
Charge.

00:09:23.720 --> 00:09:26.900
(dramatic music)

00:09:26.900 --> 00:09:29.320
The alphas must make a quick decision.

00:09:29.360 --> 00:09:32.300
Can they take down a
bull moose in his prime?

00:09:32.300 --> 00:09:35.740
Is this worth exhausting
the entire family?

00:09:36.180 --> 00:09:43.140
(dramatic music)

00:09:44.600 --> 00:09:45.560
Not this time.

00:09:49.360 --> 00:09:50.860
Better find a new target.

00:09:52.080 --> 00:09:59.780
(howling)

00:10:02.040 --> 00:10:35.120
(slow tempo music)

